• Key Q7 finding: the spx: stamps do NOT carry the tool name. They are meta values like "spx:detector" with values "integer"/"openms", etc. The `spx:` prefix is literal and unchanged. So the CHANGELOG line 7-8 — "The name reaches only the mzML header (the software entry and the `spx:` run stamps' tool name)" — is FALSE in its parenthetical: the spx: stamps carry no tool name; the name reaches only the software entry and ini section. Wait — "the `spx:` run stamps' tool name" — maybe getProcessingInfo_ includes the tool name in the DataProcessing software? The software entry is from TOPPBase (tool name). The stamps listed at 4147-4171 don't include the name. Is there any stamp with the tool name? Line 4168: dnoise stamp — no. So the CHANGELOG parenthetical is wrong. Severity: LOW (docs accuracy), and it's on the "right side" (claiming name appears in more places than it does — actually it claims the name reaches the spx: stamps, which if true would put the name INSIDE files users compare; since false, harmless but inaccurate). Note: the task says "spx: header stamps ... (inside the digest)" — wait, are the spx: stamps inside the digest? The digest hashes <spectrumList> only, so run-level userParams are excluded. The CHANGELOG line 39-40 says "the `spx:` run stamps and the `spx_guessed` / `spx_n_isotopes` precursor userParams (they sit inside the spectrum digest)" — hmm, spx_guessed/spx_n_isotopes are precursor userParams = inside spectrumList = inside digest. But the spx: RUN stamps are on the PeakMap (run level), OUTSIDE <spectrumList>? setMetaValue on out_exp (the PeakMap) → these are run-level userParams, which appear in the mzML header <userParam> under <run> — outside spectrumList. So "they sit inside the spectrum digest" is WRONG for the spx: run stamps — the semantic digest hashes only <spectrumList>..</spectrumList> (README line 208). Run-level stamps are excluded. So the CHANGELOG's parenthetical conflates: the precursor userParams (spx_guessed etc.) sit inside the digest; the run stamps do not. Wait — do the run stamps sit inside <spectrumList>? No, they're map-level. Hmm, but the sentence says "the `spx:` run stamps and the `spx_guessed` / `spx_n_isotopes` precursor userParams (they sit inside the spectrum digest)". For the run stamps that's false per README's digest description. Also line 7-8: "the name reaches only the mzML header (the software entry and the `spx:` run stamps' tool name)" — false because stamps carry no name. Both are CHANGELOG accuracy findings (LOW, but exactly what Q7 asks).

  Wait, also line 4156: spx:detector value is "integer"/"openms" — no tool name. Confirmed.

  Now .gitignore changed in d02ec71: `docs/reviews/**/*.log` → `docs/**/*.log`. This widens the ignore. Is that a problem? It means .log files anywhere under docs/ are now ignored — could hide files that should be tracked, or conversely the old pattern was insufficient. Also this is a non-rename change in a "rename only" release — the CHANGELOG claims "Nothing else in the tree changed between 1.2.1 and this release". Between 1.2.1 (62b53ea) and HEAD: f6007fe (corpus-v3.md +107), 0120485 (BACKLOG +34), bb9ee04 (BACKLOG +12), and .gitignore widening. So the statement is false in at least four ways: corpus-v3.md added, two BACKLOG additions, .gitignore widened. Although one could argue these are not "the release" content... but "Nothing else in the tree changed" is plainly contradicted. Severity LOW (docs truthfulness) — but the review brief explicitly flags it. Also .gitignore widening is a behavior change in the release commit d02ec71 — and d02ec71 is supposed to touch no build input (invariant 7: docs commit touches no build input — .gitignore is not a build input, fine for reproducibility).

  Wait — but .gitignore change could matter for export: export uses git ls-files; .gitignore doesn't affect tracked files. OK.

  Hmm, actually the .gitignore widening to docs/**/*.log: any tracked .log under docs/? git ls-files would show. Probably none. Fine.

  Now let me continue with Q9: the rest of the src diff. The diff shown so far is all identifier/string renames. Need the remaining hunks of src/diaspextract.cpp (163 changed lines). Let me get the full src/diaspextract.cpp diff and check each hunk for non-rename changes. Also test_diaspextract.py diff, scripts, check_option_tokens.py.

  Also Q1 reverse-rename check: grep for things like "DIAspeXtract" appearing where old name should remain: e.g., URLs okohlbacher/diaspextract (fine), cluster paths — check no "/ceph/ibmi/abi/oliver/spextract" was renamed (subagent D covers scripts/). Check bench/run_joint.sh.example and run_open.sh.example diffs. Let me view the diff for bench/, scripts/, harness/, .github, tests/.

  Also Q8 defaults: read registerOptionsAndFlags_ region. Let me find it.I found that the spx: stamps are meta tags like spx:detector and do not include the tool name. Therefore the changelog line claiming the tool name appears in the spx: stamps is wrong; the tool name only appears in the software entry. getProcessingInfo_ might refer to the tool name within the DataProcessing software.I found that the software entry stems from TOPPBase, but the spx: stamps at lines 4147‑4171 contain no tool name, nor does the stamp on line 4168. Therefore the changelog’s statement is incorrect, though the problem is a minor documentation accuracy issue with low severity.I realised that the changelog incorrectly says the spx: run stamps are part of the spectrum digest; in fact they’re set at the PeakMap (run) level and excluded from the digest, while only the precursor parameters like spx_guessed and spx_n_isotopes are included in the digest.The changelog incorrectly states that the `spx:` run stamps carry a tool name—actually they only contain values such as “openms”. Likewise the claim that the name reaches the mzML header via the run stamps is false; the name is only in the software entry. This is a low‑severity documentation mismatch.The .gitignore was broadened – now any .log under docs/ is ignored – which counts as a change, and along with added files and backlog entries this contradicts the changelog’s claim that “nothing else changed.” This documentation mismatch is low‑severity.I noted .gitignore isn’t a build input and that expanding it to docs/**/*.log probably affects no tracked files; the next step is to review the remaining hunks of src/diaspextract.cpp.Examining the diffs for diaspextract.cpp, test_diaspextract.py, scripts, check_option_tokens.py, bench, harness, .github, tests, searching for non‑rename changes, reverse‑rename patterns, and the registerOptionsAndFlags_ defaults.+// Copyright (c) 2002-present, OpenMS Inc. -- EKU Tuebingen, ETH Zurich, and FU Berlin
