  Also Q2's sub-question: "Could the assertion pass for the wrong reason (the guard's generic message mentions SPEXTRACTOR_* and DIASPEXTRACTOR_* on every line)?" — Answer: No. The generic text contains "SPEXTRACTOR_*" and "DIASPEXTRACTOR_*" but the assertion requires the exact variable name (e.g. "SPEXTRACTOR_DET", "DIASPEXTRACTOR_TILE_RESIDENT"), which the generic text never contains; and the lookbehind (?<![A-Z]) specifically prevents "SPEXTRACTOR_DET" matching inside "DIASPEXTRACTOR_DET". One caveat: for the "both" case, if the guard printed only the DIASPEXTRACTOR variable and its generic template, the SPEXTRACTOR_LEDGER assertion would fail — good. But subtle wrong-reason: new_name regex for "DIASPEXTRACT_DET" — the generic template says "reads only DIASPEXTRACT_* variables" — contains "DIASPEXTRACT_*" not "DIASPEXTRACT_DET". OK. Another: in case "spextractor", could "DIASPEXTRACT_DET" match from... the refusal message itself prints "rename it to DIASPEXTRACT_DET" — genuine. Also the message mentions "DIASPEXTRACT_*" — regex "DIASPEXTRACT_DET\b" needs exact. Fine. So assertion cannot pass for the wrong reason, with one microscopic exception: if the variable name were a substring of another printed name with a lowercase/digit/underscore preceding char (lookbehind only excludes A-Z). E.g. v = "SPEXTRACTOR_DET" vs a printed "XSPEXTRACTOR_DET"? X is uppercase → blocked. Preceding char lowercase can't happen for these names (they'd have to be suffixes of longer uppercase names). Names are chosen well. Also note: `(?<![A-Z])` doesn't block a preceding '_' or digit, but no printed token has those before these names. OK.

